//? Title: SPEA2: actual strengths, density and archive decisions
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Conflicting minimized objectives require a set of trade-offs rather than one
best scalar value. SPEA2 combines dominance strength with neighbor density,
retains an external archive and breeds new bounded candidates. We inspect actual
scored pools and archive identities, retain the original six-point example and
thirty-variable ZDT1 run, and state the finite-search limits.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Strength S(i) counts other pool points dominated by i. Raw fitness sums
strengths of every dominator, so raw0 identifies nondominated points. Density
penalizes crowding:

$$S(i)=|\{j:i\prec j\}|,\qquad R(i)=\sum_{j\prec i}S(j),\qquad F(i)=R(i)+\frac1{\sigma_i^{(k)}+2},\quad k=\lfloor\sqrt P\rfloor.$$
\alttext(Strength counts dominated points, raw fitness sums dominator strengths, and total fitness adds inverse kth-neighbor distance plus two.)

The kth neighbor is one-based after excluding self. The old implementation used
index k in that nonself list, selecting the k+1 neighbor; this report corrects
that off-by-one and records the resulting seeded trajectory change. If the
archive has room, dominated candidates fill by fitness. If too many raw-zero
points remain, compare sorted neighbor-distance lists lexicographically and
remove the most crowded point repeatedly. The captured victims and survivors are
actual producing decisions.

All objectives are minimized and distances use their raw scales. Rescaling an
objective can alter density and archive diversity even when dominance is
unchanged. Each density stage needs quadratic pair work/storage; repeatedly
rebuilding truncation distances can be quartic in pool size. SBX and polynomial
mutation are canonical native operators, with an explicit local seed. A finite
approximate front is not a proof of convergence.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/spea2_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/random/quality.hpp>
#include <datamunge/optim/spea2.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = dm::optim::spea2_select_trace({{0, 1}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}}, 2);
    std::print("| actual pool index | objectives | strength | raw | density | fitness |\n| ---: | --- | " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &p : r.pool)
        std::print("| {} | `{}` | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", p.pool_index, p.f,
                   p.strength, p.raw, p.density, p.fitness);
    std::print("\nActual selected `{}`, actual truncated `{}`.\n", r.selected,
               r.truncated);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Three minimized objective points");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual dominance tests",
                                     r.dominance_tests));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Actual survivors {}", r.selected));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/spea2_tiny.svg");
}
```
